To make the content area sit next to the menu bar, you usually need to adjust the CSS layout, not the WordPress menu itself.

If your menu is currently above the content, and you want the content div to appear beside it, look for the main wrapper and content/sidebar styles in your theme’s CSS, then set them to float side by side.

Typical fix:

```css
#menu {
    float: left;
    width: 200px;
}

#content {
    float: left;
    width: 700px;
}
```

If your theme uses a wrapper like `#wrapper`, `#content-area`, `#main`, or `#sidebar`, then apply the float/width to the actual content container.

Also make sure the parent container clears the floats, for example:

```css
#wrapper:after {
    content: "";
    display: block;
    clear: both;
}
```

If you want the menu bar from the resolved thread to link to other places on the same domain, that part is handled in WordPress by adding custom links in **Appearance > Menus** or by editing the theme if it doesn’t support menus. But for getting the content next to the menu, it’s a CSS issue.

If you want, paste the relevant HTML/CSS for your content and menu divs, and I can tell you the exact change.

To place `#content_area` next to `#menu_bar`, make both elements float left and give them explicit widths that fit within the container.

Try this:

```css
#menu_bar {
    float: left;
    margin-left: 0.5%;
    margin-right: 0.5%;
    margin-top: 0.5%;
    margin-bottom: 1%;
    border: 1px solid #46A5E0;
    width: 20%;
    height: 40%;
}

#content_area {
    float: left;
    margin-left: 0.5%;
    margin-right: 0.5%;
    margin-top: 0.5%;
    margin-bottom: 1%;
    border: 1px solid #46A5E0;
    width: 78%; /* adjust as needed */
    height: 40%;
}
```

Also, if these are inside the container, make sure the total widths plus margins do not exceed the container width.

If you want the container to wrap around the floated elements properly, add a clearfix to the parent:

```css
#container::after {
    content: "";
    display: block;
    clear: both;
}
```

Why this happens:
- `div` elements are block-level by default, so they stack vertically.
- `float: left;` makes them sit side by side.

