If you want a one-page site where clicking a jump link hides all other content and shows only the matching section, the basic idea is:

- give each section an ID
- make links point to those IDs
- when a link is clicked, hide all sections with `display:none`
- show only the target section

Example:

```html
<nav>
  <a href="#topic1" class="jump">Topic 1</a>
  <a href="#topic2" class="jump">Topic 2</a>
  <a href="#topic3" class="jump">Topic 3</a>
</nav>

<section id="topic1" class="panel">Content for topic 1</section>
<section id="topic2" class="panel">Content for topic 2</section>
<section id="topic3" class="panel">Content for topic 3</section>
```

```css
.panel {
  display: none;
}
.panel.active {
  display: block;
}
```

```html
<script>
  function showPanel(id) {
    document.querySelectorAll('.panel').forEach(panel => {
      panel.classList.remove('active');
    });

    const target = document.getElementById(id);
    if (target) {
      target.classList.add('active');
    }
  }

  document.querySelectorAll('.jump').forEach(link => {
    link.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
      e.preventDefault();
      const id = this.getAttribute('href').substring(1);
      showPanel(id);
      history.replaceState(null, '', '#' + id);
    });
  });

  // Show panel on initial page load if hash exists
  if (location.hash) {
    showPanel(location.hash.substring(1));
  } else {
    showPanel('topic1'); // default section
  }
</script>
```

How it works:
- `display:none` hides all sections
- clicking a link prevents the normal jump
- JavaScript reveals only the selected section
- the URL hash still updates, so links can be shared/bookmarked

